                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "30 minutes for main story (visual novel), 30 minutes for epilogue (visual novel), and an hour for extras achievement cleanup (photos, short stories, and album).\n\nMain story was kind of boring.  The conversation didn't go anywhere (you spend an inordinate amount of time discussing the dad's tomato garden), it was basically a bunch of surface level small talk and then the story suddenly ends.  Weird gameplay choice in forcing you to hold down right trigger for entire game to progress dialogue (if you let go, everything halts until you hold it again).  Even more bizarre choice to make the rain effects obscure text, inducing unnecessary eyestrain (I had to close my eyes multiple times to rest).  You also have to annoyingly bounce your focus back and forth from left side of screen (phone dialogue) to right (your responses), which is like... why?  Furthermore something like half of your responses are \"...\", which a) as tiny dots get obscured by aforementioned rain, so often difficult to tell when your prompt has arrived vs. waiting for phone dialogue to finish; b) puts you into autopilot mode which led to repeated cases of me accidentally selecting a non \"...\" response that finally arrived after a long string of \"...\".\n\nEpilogue was significantly better, with much tighter focus on deeper conversation between mother and daughter about life problems, and things spoken (or left regretfully unspoken).  The gameplay issues mentioned above have been more or less mitigated.  You can run this 3 times for the good ending, bad ending, and (brief) joke ending, in all totalling 30 minutes.\n\nAchievement hunters will need to spend the final hour exploring the extras.  There is a pretty solid (IMO) black and white photography school project as mentioned in the epilogue.  Her professor giving her a C- was nonsense!  Then there are the brother's short stories, all of which were unfortunately highly pretentious and painful to read.  Finally the bulk of the cleanup time will be spent on listening to 12 tracks of background music, which was... okay I guess.\n\nIn conclusion, about 15 minutes of worthwhile material (one run of the epilogue) surrounded by way too much chaff.",
